<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mysia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mysia is a historical region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, lying south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a> and bordering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troad">Troad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>. In antiquity it included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, capital of the Attalid kingdom, and later formed part of the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Around 1300 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> Karasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> took most of the region from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> before passing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mysia.jpg" alt="Mysia" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HALUK COMERTEL · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bergama_-_AKRAPOL_-_panoramio_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
